  "question": {
    "text": "The ratio of surface tensions of mercury and water is given to be <math><mn>7.5</mn></math>, while the ratio of their densities is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>13.6</mn></math>.&nbsp;Their contact angles, with glass, are close to <math><mn>135</mn><mo>°</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>°</mo></math>, respectively. If it is observed that mercury gets depressed by an amount <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> in a capillary tube of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>, while water rises by the same amount <math><mi>h</mi></math> in a capillary tube of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, then&nbsp;the ratio&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math>&nbsp;is close to",
    "image": null
  },
